   "summary": "Every commenter in this sample opposes the proposed shift to electronic delivery of group health plan notices as the default. They argue that treating inaction as consent is not real consent and that people should affirmatively choose electronic delivery. Many say seniors, rural residents, lower-income households, people with disabilities, military members and security-wary people would be hurt most, because they would need to navigate opt-out steps across several insurers. Others see the rule as driven by insurer cost savings rather than consumer benefit, and say there is no evidence that people switched automatically will read and understand the notices. Most ask that the current opt-in framework stay, with paper as the default, and a few short comments simply say they depend on paper mail.",
